module std.tui

// Drawing a frame without redrawing what has not changed.
//
// The library's standing advice — build the whole frame as one string and `print` it once
// — is what `screen.lyra` and `text.lyra` are shaped around, and it is right as far as it
// goes. What it leaves on the table is that most frames differ from the one before by very
// little: a viewer where only the status line moves rewrites every gradient cell on the
// screen to change six characters. `Renderer` keeps the last frame and writes only the
// rows that differ, still in a single `print`.
//
// **The diff is by row, and that is a limit rather than a shortcut.** A row string carries
// escape sequences — colour, attributes — and nothing here can tell which of its runes are
// visible cells and which are part of a sequence; `text.lyra`'s header makes the same point
// about `fit`. A per-cell diff would need a parser for every sequence a caller might use
// and a cell model to diff against, which is a different library. A per-row diff needs
// neither, and takes the cost of a frame from "every cell" to "the rows that moved", which
// is the difference that shows.

/// A frame writer that remembers what is on the screen.
///
/// Construct one, hand each frame to `render`, and it writes the difference. It holds no
/// terminal state of its own — `alt_screen_enter`, `cursor_hide` and the rest are still the
/// caller's to pair — only the rows it last wrote.
pub struct Renderer {
  /// The rows as last written to the screen. Empty means nothing is known to be there, so
  /// the next frame is written whole.
  prev: []string,
  /// Scratch: the bytes of the frame being written, reused across frames.
  ///
  /// It lives here rather than in `render` because a local one is a fresh box, a fresh
  /// buffer and a growth realloc **per frame** — which is most of what a small frame costs.
  /// Cleared at the top of each render and refilled, so after the first frame the memory is
  /// already there and already big enough. It holds nothing between calls: `decode_utf8`
  /// copies out, so what is left here is capacity, not state.
  buf: []u8,
}

/// A renderer that assumes nothing is on the screen, so the first frame is written whole.
pub let renderer = pure () -> Renderer => Renderer { prev: [], buf: [] }

/// Write the rows that differ from the previous frame, in one `print`.
///
/// ```lyra
/// var r = renderer()
/// for _ in 0..<frames {
///   var rows: []string = []
///   for y in 0..<rows_count { rows.push(paint_row(y, cols)) }
///   rows.push(status_bar("q quits", cols))
///   r.render(rows)
/// }
/// ```
///
/// **Consecutive changed rows share one cursor move.** The measurement this library is
/// built on is that positioning per cell costs more than the text does; positioning per
/// *row* is the same mistake one order of magnitude down, and a frame where the top half
/// scrolls would pay it on every row. A run of adjacent changed rows is written as one
/// position escape followed by the rows joined with `\r\n`, so the common cases — one row
/// changed, or a contiguous band — cost one move each.
///
/// **Every row must be the full width of the screen.** A row shorter than the one it
/// replaces leaves the tail of the old one on screen, which is `fit`'s reason for
/// existing. This deliberately does *not* append an erase-to-end-of-line: that erases with
/// the **current background colour**, so on a row that ends inside a coloured span it
/// paints the rest of the line in that colour — a worse artifact than the one it fixes,
/// and one that appears only on the frames where colour is used.
///
/// # Panics
/// Never traps. A frame with more or fewer rows than the last is handled by writing the
/// rows that are new; see `invalidate` for what a *resize* needs on top of that.
pub let render = (self: mut Renderer, rows: []string) -> void => {
  // **Bytes, not `++`.** A string is immutable, so accumulating one copies everything
  // written so far at every step and a whole frame costs O(size²); a `[]u8` grows by
  // doubling, so the same bytes cost O(size) across every reallocation together. It is the
  // shape `join` uses, and for the same reason — this file just cannot call `join`, since
  // the pieces are decided one at a time by the diff rather than being an array to begin
  // with. (Measured, and collecting the pieces into a `[]string` to hand to `join` was
  // *slower* than `++` at 60x200: the piece array costs more at that size than the
  // asymptotics save.)
  //
  // **No longer a trade: it is faster on both axes.** Four versions of this loop have been
  // measured against each other, one row changing in a 60-row frame and a full 120x400
  // redraw:
  //
  //   `++` per row               1825 ns   115 us
  //   `push_utf8` per byte       1921 ns    11 us
  //   `push_utf8` bulk           1921 ns    11 us
  //   bulk into a reused buffer  1788 ns    10 us
  //
  // The last line is `self.buf` plus `clear`, and it is what finally closed a regression the
  // middle two carried: for a 200-byte row the byte path was never losing on *copying* — one
  // memcpy beat two concatenations from the start — it was losing on **allocation count**, a
  // fresh box and buffer and a growth realloc every frame against two string boxes. Reusing
  // the buffer removes all three, and what is left per steady-state frame is the position
  // string and the decode.
  //
  // The lesson is worth more than the 2%: three rounds of making the copy faster did not
  // reach a cost that was never copying. The full redraw is 11x, and was already 10x before
  // the reuse.
  self.buf.clear()
  let n = rows.len()
  for i in 0..<n {
    if self.row_changed(i, rows[i]) {
      // Start a run here, then extend it over every following row that also changed. The
      // position is written once for the whole run.
      self.buf.push_utf8(at(0, i))
      for j in i..<n {
        if !self.row_changed(j, rows[j]) { break }
        if j > i { self.buf.push_utf8("\r\n") }
        self.buf.push_utf8(rows[j])
        self.mark(j, rows[j])
      }
    }
  }
  self.truncate(n)
  // An unchanged frame writes nothing, exactly as before — `at` never returns the empty
  // string, so an empty buffer means no run was ever started. The empty `[]u8` it allocated
  // to find that out is the one thing this costs a no-change frame, against the `print` it
  // still skips.
  if self.buf.len() > 0 { print(self.buf.decode_utf8()) }
}

/// Forget what is on the screen, so the next `render` writes the whole frame.
///
/// Needed after anything that changes the screen behind the renderer's back — `clear()`,
/// leaving and re-entering the alternate buffer, and above all a **resize**, where the
/// terminal reflows what was there and the remembered rows no longer describe it. A
/// program that redraws on `terminal_size` changing should call this in the same branch.
pub let invalidate = (self: mut Renderer) -> void => { self.prev = [] }

/// Whether row `i` differs from what was last written there. A row past the end of the
/// previous frame counts as changed, which is what makes a first frame write in full.
let row_changed = pure (self: Renderer, i: i64, row: string) -> bool =>
  i >= self.prev.len() || self.prev[i] != row

/// Record what row `i` now holds, growing the record if this frame is taller than the last.
let mark = (self: mut Renderer, i: i64, row: string) -> void => {
  if i < self.prev.len() { self.prev[i] = row } else { self.prev.push(row) }
}

/// Drop the record of any row beyond `n`, so a frame that got shorter does not compare
/// against rows it no longer draws. What is still *on* the screen there is the caller's to
/// erase — the renderer writes rows, it does not own the screen.
let truncate = (self: mut Renderer, n: i64) -> void => {
  if self.prev.len() <= n { return }
  var kept: []string = []
  for i in 0..<n { kept.push(self.prev[i]) }
  self.prev = kept
}

/// The escape sequence that puts the cursor at `col`, `row` — **0-based, column first**,
/// exactly as `move_to`, which is this printed.
///
/// The returning half exists because a diff has to compose positioning *into* the string
/// it prints once; `move_to` performs its sequence, which is right for placing a cursor and
/// useless for building a frame. It is the same split `style.lyra` and `screen.lyra` draw,
/// and the `+ 1`s that reconcile the terminal's 1-based numbering live here alone —
/// `move_to` is now `print(at(col, row))`.
pub let at = pure (col: i64, row: i64) -> string => "\e[${row + 1};${col + 1}H"
